    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "12V vs 18V Drill",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/12v-vs-18v-drill/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a 12V drill good enough for home use?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most indoor household work, yes. A good 12V drill assembles furniture, hangs shelves and brackets, installs cabinet hardware and drills small holes in wood and metal easily. It falls short on large hole saws, long screws without pilot holes, deck building and concrete. If those jobs are rare for you, a 12V drill is a sensible single drill."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a 12V drill strong enough to drill into studs?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, for normal pilot holes and screws. Driving 2 to 2.5 inch screws into wood studs to mount shelves or a TV bracket is well within a 12V drill's range, especially with a pilot hole. Boring large spade-bit or hole-saw holes through several studs for cable or pipe is where an 18V drill does noticeably better."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is 12V vs 18V the same as 12V vs 20V?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Drills sold as 20V MAX use the same five-cell battery as 18V drills; the brand simply quotes peak rather than nominal voltage. So comparing a 12V drill with a 20V MAX drill is the same comparison as 12V against 18V: three battery cells against five, with the same trade-offs in weight, power and runtime."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do 12V drills have hammer drill versions?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Some 12V platforms include a compact hammer drill, and it can handle small holes in brick, mortar and block for wall plugs. For poured concrete or larger anchors, an 18V hammer drill is far quicker, and frequent concrete work calls for a rotary hammer. Check the specific platform, because not every 12V line offers a hammer version."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a 12V drill mix paint or thinset?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is not a good idea. Mixing paddles put a long, heavy load on the motor, and many 12V drills have 3/8 inch chucks that do not fit full-size paddle shanks. A small paint stir in a quart can is fine. For buckets of paint, mortar or thinset, use an 18V drill in low gear or a dedicated mixer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which is better for a beginner, 12V or 18V?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the projects. A beginner who mostly assembles furniture and hangs things will find a 12V drill lighter and easier to control. A beginner who plans outdoor builds or wants to add saws and yard tools later should start on an 18V or 20V MAX platform. When unsure, the 18V class covers more future jobs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does a 12V battery fit an 18V drill with an adapter?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Within the major brands, 12V and 18V packs are separate systems, and the manufacturers do not sell adapters to run one class on the other. Third-party adapters for cross-class use are uncommon and not something we recommend, because they bypass the protection designed into matched packs and tools. Buy batteries for the class each tool uses."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
