      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Pick a 12V drill if most of your work is assembling furniture, hanging shelves and curtain rods, installing cabinet hardware or working in tight spaces, because it is about a pound lighter, smaller and still strong enough for those jobs. Pick an 18V drill, or a 20V MAX drill, which is the same class, if you build decks and fences, bore large holes through framing, drill into brick or concrete, or want one battery that also runs saws and yard tools. The 18V drill has more torque, more runtime per charge and a far bigger tool family; the 12V drill is easier to hold all day and fits where the big one will not. That is why so many people who use their tools often end up owning one of each.
        </p>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Should You Buy a 12V or an 18V Drill?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The honest answer depends on the heaviest job you expect to do, not the average one. A 12V drill handles the bulk of household tasks without strain: driving screws up to a couple of inches into softwood, drilling pilot holes, assembling flat-pack furniture, mounting brackets and installing hardware. An 18V drill handles all of that plus the jobs a 12V drill struggles with, such as large spade bits and hole saws, long screws into hardwood without pilot holes, and masonry if it has a hammer function.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only want one drill and you are not sure what you will tackle, the 18V class is the safer bet, because it covers the hard jobs and its platform usually includes everything from circular saws to leaf blowers. If you already know your work is light and indoor, or you are buying for an apartment, a small home or someone who finds a full-size drill tiring, a 12V drill is the better tool rather than a compromise.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
