        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A 12V drill is the lighter, smaller and more precise tool, strong enough for furniture, cabinets, hardware and most indoor jobs. An 18V or 20V MAX drill brings more torque, longer runtime, a 1/2 inch chuck and a much broader battery platform, which is what decks, big holes, masonry and yard tools need. Buy for your hardest likely job, not your most common one.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you can only have one, the 18V class covers more ground for most homeowners. If you already have one, adding the other class later is the upgrade many regular DIYers end up making. Our cordless drill rankings cover the best options at both sizes.
          </p>
